<p>The idea of wanting to do hard, important work with great people is common enough to be a platitude. But when I try to articulate what makes me feel most alive, that's basically it. Difficulty, importance, ownership, people. The domain doesn't seem to matter; I've felt this doing research, software, events, art. It's the shape of the work, not the content.</p>

<p>Difficulty can be genuinely hard work but also tight deadlines, seemingly impossible constraints, decisions under uncertainty, context that requires hours to understand. Things that I struggle to hold in my hand but seek to, things that we make theories about to <a href="/posts/compression">compress</a> them.</p>

<p>Importance: does this reach people? Does it matter? Even the <em>feeling</em> of importance counts: I'm not above being motivated by narrative, something difficulty often adds. To be aware of.</p>

<p>Ownership means building over fixing over commenting. Am I uniquely placed to do this? If not, why me? This is probably where the deepest purpose actually lives, and is again highly linked with importance. If work is important and I am good at it, it is probably something I am well placed to do.</p>

<p>And I want to work with people I like. We all do. This is more controllable than it seems — I can choose teams I like, start orgs myself, or work on becoming someone who can appreciate more kinds of people.</p>

<p style="text-align: center;">⁂</p>

<p>I notice that ownership and learning are different modes. In learning mode, you're a stepping stone toward something — the grand vision matters less, you're accumulating. In ownership mode, purpose becomes load-bearing. I'm in learning mode now. The goal is to get to ownership mode faster. Eventually, everything I do should be high-ownership. The learning doesn't stop, but it's no longer the point.</p>

<p style="text-align: center;">⁂</p>

<p>I think my relationship to work was shaped by how I grew up. Twelve years in California meant tech was in front of my face all the time, no other paths really visible, at least not as a kid. I naturally took to it. I loved being able to create little interfaces, experimenting with animation and art-meets-tech stuff. My life was mainly about learning more and making more. It was a simple time. I believed learning was the most purposeful thing one could be doing! I was nerdy about math and science and stars and anime and drawing.</p>

<p>Then London. Completely different culture. Still a strong culture of learning for its own sake, but in a different way: more theoretical, more aesthetically driven. More discerning about where technology was deployed, too much so. In high school, I was one of 2 students who took CS and I was extremely lucky to have a great CS teacher in high school who shaped my love for the subject by pushing me to explore and create.</p>

<p>Now Oxford, final year. I find loads of mediums and topics exciting across disciplines. I don't think it's the content that drives my passion: it's the difficulty and importance. Does this reach a lot of people or have a large impact? I notice I feel more alive when I am building over fixing. I like working to difficult deadlines and making decisions around seemingly impossible constraints.</p>

<p style="text-align: center;">⁂</p>

<p>Some frames I'm holding loosely, from recent conversations:</p>

<p>Mentorship (on either side) as a purpose-generator (to use and be aware of). Professorship as a way to stay active, relevant, and social as you age. Keeping the door to leave always open. Jobs shape your values even if you started out quite different-- be aware. Values as things you choose (when you're leading) versus things that get chosen for you (when you're in an org). It's okay to be on either side, but you have to know which one you're in.</p>
